  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Giens peninsula, in Hyères, offers a coastal trail that runs along wild coves, with sea views at every turn of the path. The full loop ("sentier du littoral + route des crêtes") is about 10.5 km for 3h to 3h30 of walking and +322 m total elevation gain; shorter variants exist, such as Darboussières ↔ Madrague (~7 km), handy if you're after a quicker outing with your dog.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The level is easy to moderate: good shoes are recommended for the rocky, sometimes slippery sections, and it's best avoided in strong wind or rain (some sections overlook the cliff). An important update on dogs: the official "Sentier du littoral" topoguide published by the Toulon Provence Méditerranée (TPM) metropolitan authority — which manages this trail, and is directly linked from Hyères town hall's own website — states plainly in its shared rules: "dogs are banned on the sentier du littoral and in Port-Cros national park." That ban officially covers the Giens peninsula loop, just as it does the other TPM member towns (Six-Fours, La Seyne, Toulon...). In practice, several online accounts mention dogs seen on a leash on this trail without it being systematically enforced — but the written rule is a ban, not a leash recommendation. If you want to walk your dog in the area, head instead to the <a href="/en/fiche-hyeres-merou">Mérou dog beach</a> in Hyères, where there is no such ambiguity. Shade varies a lot by section: more generous on the pine-wood side of La Madrague and Les Darboussières, almost absent on the rockier, more exposed western corniche. No water point is reported along the route.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: out of respect for the TPM regulation, this trail is not officially a place to walk your dog — head instead to the Mérou dog beach or Le Pradet's Cani Plouf for an outing with no risk of a fine.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Hyères and its beaches are nearby, a good option to end the day with a swim, notably the <a href="/en/fiche-hyeres-merou">Mérou dog beach</a>. On site, a <a href="/en/fiche-toiletteur-pat-toutou">groomer</a> and the <a href="/en/fiche-camping-mediterranee-hyeres">Camping Méditerranée</a> round out a stay in the area nicely.</p>
    </div>

